# Enterprise compliance contract (US Government deployments)

These products are sold to United States Federal customers. The five
requirements below are **non-negotiable**. Failing any one of them is a
release blocker, not a backlog item.

| Requirement | Scope | Authority |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **US English** in all source, comments, log messages, error strings, doc comments, UI strings, OpenAPI text, CLI help, Cargo metadata | All crates in `c:\analog-pim\*` | Project standard; localization layers on top via i18n |
| **Section 508 / WCAG 2.0 AA** | Every GUI surface (Win32, Tauri, web UI) | 29 U.S.C. § 794d, 36 CFR Part 1194 |
| **NIST SP 800-53 Rev 5** moderate baseline | All security-relevant paths (auth, IPC, key management, audit logging, logging, configuration storage, network) | FedRAMP / agency ATO |
| **FIPS 140-3** validated cryptography | Every cryptographic operation protecting sensitive data at rest, in transit, or in process memory | NIST FIPS 140-3 |
| **Audit logging of every security-relevant operator action** | Every CLI subcommand and GUI action that mutates state, authorizes access, or surfaces secrets | NIST 800-53 AU-2, AU-3, AU-9, AU-12 |

## 1. US English (development language)

Every identifier, comment, doc comment, log message, `expect` message,
error string, OpenAPI description, CLI help text, and `Cargo.toml`
description uses American English spelling.

Replace on sight: behaviour→behavior, colour→color, honoured→honored,
initialise→initialize, recognise→recognize, customise→customize,
synchronise→synchronize, analyse→analyze, organise→organize,
optimise→optimize, minimise→minimize, maximise→maximize,
normalise→normalize, finalise→finalize, authorise→authorize (and their
-d / -ing forms), defence→defense, offence→offense, licence (verb)→
license, centre→center, catalogue→catalog, dialogue→dialog (UI only;
"dialogue" is fine in legal text), programme→program, whilst→while,
grey→gray.

**For end-user text:** source strings stay US English. Translation
happens through the i18n layer — never hand-translate a string in source.
Compliance artifacts (reports, evidence, mappings, legal notices,
dashboards) are **must-localize** in every Tier 1 language — see
[`compliance-artifacts-must-localize.mdc`](compliance-artifacts-must-localize.mdc).
Quality and register for all product localization:
[`enterprise-localization.mdc`](enterprise-localization.mdc).

## 2. Section 508 / WCAG 2.0 AA

Every GUI surface — Win32 dialogs, Tauri shells, the web UI in
`pim-offline-server/ui` — must conform to Section 508 and the underlying
WCAG 2.0 AA criteria.

Detailed Win32 + Tauri contracts:
[`pim-offline-client/AGENTS.md`](../../pim-offline-client/AGENTS.md#section-508--accessibility-contract-win32--tauri-operator-uis).
Run `pim-offline-client/scripts/audit-section-508.ps1` on every `.rc`
change. The brand image must be the real AIC mark, never the noise
placeholder — see [`no-noise-desktop-icons.mdc`](no-noise-desktop-icons.mdc).

## 3. NIST SP 800-53 Rev 5 (moderate baseline)

The moderate baseline is the floor.

| Family | Examples | Where enforced |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **AC** Access Control | AC-2, AC-6, AC-17 | `pim-offline-server/src/api/*`, `pim-offline-client/src/elevation/*` |
| **AU** Audit and Accountability | AU-2, AU-3, AU-6, AU-9, AU-12 | §5 below — non-negotiable |
| **CM** Configuration Management | CM-3, CM-6 | `pim-app-config`, `pim-config-storage` |
| **IA** Identification and Authentication | IA-2, IA-5 | `pim-offline-server/src/api/identity.rs`, agent IPC `requesting_sid` |
| **SC** System and Communications Protection | SC-8, SC-12, SC-13, SC-17, SC-28 | TLS stack, key wrapping, FIPS provider — §4 |
| **SI** System and Information Integrity | SI-7, SI-10 | Authenticode signing, `pim-offline-client/src/validation/` |

Concrete rules contributors hit most often:

- No new HTTP route merges without its OpenAPI entry (enforced by
  `tests/openapi_coverage.rs`).
- No new admin route may omit a `security:` block in the OpenAPI spec.
- No code path constructs a TLS client/server outside the central TLS
  factory (`pim-offline-client/src/http_client/client.rs::HttpClient` and
  the server equivalent), so the FIPS provider stays in force.
- Anything touching a secret at rest goes through `pim-config-storage` —
  never environment variables, never `tempfile`-derived paths.

## 4. FIPS 140-3 cryptography

All hashing, HMAC, AEAD, key agreement, and random-byte generation flows
through a **FIPS 140-3-validated cryptographic module**, under a
**two-track posture** matched to each binary's deployment profile:

| Track | Binaries | Module | CMVP cert |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **A (server-class)** rustls + aws-lc-rs FIPS build | `pim-offline-server`, all four `pim-offline-{client,server}-configurator-{win32,tauri}` | AWS-LC Cryptographic Module (aws-lc-rs ≥ 1.5, FIPS build) | **#4759** — build-profile verification pending |
| **B (endpoint)** OS-native TLS | `pim-offline-client` (agent), `pim-offline-client-elevate-{win32,tauri}` | Windows SChannel / `BCryptPrimitives.dll`; macOS Apple corecrypto; Linux distro OpenSSL (FIPS only where the distro ships one, e.g. RHEL 8/9) | Windows #4515 / #4694, Apple #4748 / #4751, RHEL 9 #4746 — exact SKU pins pending |

Agents run on heterogeneous endpoint hardware and use OS-certified crypto
so validation rides on the platform vendor's CMVP cert. Server and
configurators are deployment-controlled hosts where we ship a uniform
aws-lc-rs FIPS module ourselves.

**Provider installation.** Every shipping `main()` calls
`pim_crypto_rustls_bridge::install_default_provider()` as its first
statement. The bridge crate is
`pim-app-config/crates/pim-crypto-rustls-bridge/`; its `fips_status()`
API surfaces module + cert for the configurator's "About crypto" panel
and per-binary smoke tests.

`ring` is **forbidden** as a primary crypto provider in shipping crates;
it is permitted only in test code the binary never links.

### FIPS posture is HARD-CODED, not a Cargo feature

The `fips` Cargo feature was **removed** from
`pim-crypto-rustls-bridge`, `pim-windows-oplog`, `pim-offline-server`,
`pim-offline-client`, and the six sibling configurator / elevation
crates. `rustls` declares `features = [… "fips" …]` and `aws-lc-rs`
declares `default-features = false, features = ["fips"]` directly in
`[dependencies]`. `FipsStatus::NonFips` was deleted from the enum, and
`fips_status()` returns `Validated { cert_number: "4759", … }` with no
`#[cfg]` branching.

> **There is no Cargo feature, environment variable, runtime switch, or
> `--no-default-features` invocation that can produce a non-FIPS build of
> any shipping AIC Offline binary.** A PR that reintroduces a non-FIPS
> code path — as a feature, a `cfg` gate, or an alternate backend
> selector — is a release blocker.

### Customer-facing FIPS posture (what we may claim today)

Literal-truth claims only. Endpoint agent and elevation utilities rely on
the **host OS** FIPS 140-3 validated TLS stack (SChannel #4515 / #4694,
Apple corecrypto, distro OpenSSL where the distro ships a validated
build) — validation tracks the OS vendor's cert. Server and both
configurators install AWS-LC as the rustls `CryptoProvider` at startup,
targeting CMVP **#4759**; the shipped build's module version and status
come from `fips_status()` at runtime and the configurator's "About
crypto" panel. **Never claim "the entire binary is CMVP-certified"** —
only the crypto module boundary is #4759, and residual non-FIPS
`aws-lc-sys` objects may remain as dead code from upstream crates.

Measured per-binary dependency state, honesty caveats, and the FIPS
completion checklist live in
`core-assets/docs/dev/rule-enforcement-status.md`. Re-run
`cargo tree -i ring --target all` after any dependency or feature change.

### Application crypto: routing and the provider seam

Application crypto splits into two groups, only one of which is ever
delegated to a non-default provider:

- **Provider-routable** — server-side AES-GCM at rest (database column
  encryption, settings wrapping, audit-log signing), enrollment-token MAC
  verification when the MAC key lives in an HSM, server token signing
  keys, KEK custody, randomness for new master keys. These call sites
  MUST go through the `CryptoAdapter` trait so the provider can be
  swapped at runtime. The default in-process implementation calls
  `aws_lc_rs::*`, so a deployment with no HSM still produces a
  CMVP-validated result.
- **Always-local** — wire-protocol HMAC-SHA256 over canonical request
  bytes, body SHA-256 for canonical hashing, `redact_blob` audit
  fingerprints, and in-flight PBKDF2 of a user-supplied password (the
  plaintext is already in process; delegating buys nothing). These stay
  on direct `aws_lc_rs::*` calls and share the TLS FIPS module boundary.

> **Every adapter in the catalog must itself carry a CMVP cert** — or be
> on the CMVP Implementation Under Test / Modules In Process list and be
> reported as such by `FipsStatus::PendingValidation`. Adapters with no
> FIPS validation cannot be offered, even as a dev-only option.

**Agent posture is unchanged.** Agents never see HSMs; their entire
application-crypto surface stays on the in-process AWS-LC FIPS module
with the wire on OS-certified `native-tls`. The provider seam is
server-only.

### Password hashing: PBKDF2, not Argon2id

Argon2id was retired (May 2026) because it is not on the NIST CMVP
approved-algorithm list and has no implementation inside any
FIPS-validated module; routing password hashing through it would force a
qualified FIPS claim that Federal procurement reviewers reject.

| Requirement | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Algorithm | PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA-256 (NIST SP 800-132 §5.2) via `aws_lc_rs::pbkdf2` |
| Iterations | 600_000 (OWASP 2023 floor) |
| Salt | 16 bytes from `aws_lc_rs::rand::SystemRandom` |
| Output | 32 bytes |
| Storage format | `pbkdf2-sha256$<iterations>$<b64url-nopad salt>$<b64url-nopad hash>` |
| DB enforcement | `pim-orm-offline` CHECK constraints `chk_enrollment_tokens_hash_phc`, `chk_offline_tenants_secret_hash_phc` enforce the `pbkdf2-sha256$` prefix |
| Legacy `$argon2id$` rows | Rejected as `InvalidStoredHash` — a loud "rotate this secret" audit event, not a generic 401 |

**No backward-compatibility verify path is provided.** The product is at
`1.0.0-rc.1` with no production customers, so a clean break is
acceptable; post-GA any algorithm change must follow a documented
rotation window.

**Until the FIPS checklist completes:** key material lives in
`pim_offline_client::secret_storage::SecureString` (zeroized on drop) or
the OS keyring — never environment variables, never a plain `String`
past the constructor. Server-side TLS listeners accept only
FIPS-approved cipher suites, not "modern default". Crypto is
deterministic to compile-time configuration; no runtime flag may
downgrade to a non-FIPS provider. If an upstream crate cannot be steered
onto a FIPS provider, raise the dependency upgrade as a release blocker,
not a workaround.

## 5. Audit logging — hard requirement

> Every security-relevant operator action must produce a Windows Event
> Log entry (and on Linux/macOS a `tracing`-based audit log to
> `/var/log/aic/<product>/audit.log` with the same payload shape).

"Security-relevant" includes: any elevation request submission (success,
failure, format rejection, and agent-unreachable); any configurator
action that installs, uninstalls, starts, or stops the agent / server
service; any operation that sets, rotates, or clears a secret in the
configuration store; any settings import / export; any operator-triggered
connectivity or diagnostics probe; and any administrator action surfaced
through the web UI's admin API (additionally recorded in the SQL `audit_*`
tables).

**Suite-wide channel requirement (AU-2 / AU-3 / AU-9 / AU-12):** every
shipping binary emits via `pim-windows-oplog` on Windows **and**
implements RFC 5424 syslog when configured. Tracing-only event-id stubs
and unused syslog keys are forbidden — see
[`suite-observability-wel-syslog.mdc`](suite-observability-wel-syslog.mdc).

### Event payload contract

| Field | Source |
| --- | --- |
| **Actor** | Windows token name + SID of the invoking OS user (`GetTokenInformation` / `LookupAccountSidW`) |
| **Action** | A stable verb (`service_install`, `elevation_submit`, `secret_set`, …) |
| **Target** | The object acted on by **name**, never by value (service name, secret key name) |
| **Outcome** | `success` or `failure: <reason>` in US English |
| **Request id** | UUID v4 from the utility; on elevation flows also sent in the IPC request so the agent's GRANT / DENY event correlates to the UI's SUBMIT event |
| **Source surface** | Utility name + version (`pim-offline-elevate-tauri v1.0.4`) |
| **Timestamp** | UTC ISO-8601 embedded in the message for cross-host correlation |

### Masking contract (NEVER log these values)

| Value | Masking rule |
| --- | --- |
| Password | `[password len=N]` — never the value, not even the first character |
| Activation code | `[code:Xy*** len=8]` — at most first 2 chars + length |
| Bearer / API token | `[token:abc1***wxyz len=N]` — first 4 + last 4 + length |
| Database URL | Password component stripped (`redact_database_url`) |
| Private key bytes | `[redacted-pem len=N type=<RSA|EC|...>]` |
| settings.json contents | Hash + length only, never raw bytes |
| PII (email, phone, `requesting_caller` hostname) | Pass-through allowed; hostname is not a secret |

Masking lives in `pim_app_config::windows_oplog::redact::*`. Every call
site MUST use the shared helpers — never inline `.replace()` masking.

### Event ID allocation (stable; SIEM filters key on these)

| Range | Owner |
| --- | --- |
| `41001 – 41099` | Generic process / lifecycle (`pim-windows-oplog::ids`) |
| `4400 – 4499` | Agent IPC elevation (GRANT / DENY / MISSING_IDENTITY) |
| `4500 – 4599` | Elevate UI submit events (Win32 + Tauri) |
| `4600 – 4699` | Client configurator actions (Win32 + Tauri + CLI) |
| `4700 – 4799` | Server configurator actions (Win32 + Tauri + CLI) |
| `4800 – 4899` | Server admin API actions echoed to the OS event log |

A new ID requires bumping the message catalog
(`pim-windows-oplog-messages/oplog_messages.mc`) **and** registering it
in `declared_event_ids()` so the runtime self-test round-trips it.

### Audit failure handling

Audit writes must never block the user-visible operation. If
`ReportEventW` (or the Unix audit file) fails, record the failure to the
diagnostic log and proceed. **A silent audit failure is still an AU-9
violation** — the diagnostic log entry is the recovery trail.

## 6. Commit hygiene (FedRAMP audit trail)

A reviewer reading `git log -p -- src/api/admin_server_identity.rs` must
understand why each line changed without spelunking unrelated history.

1. **One concern per commit.** Refactors, documentation sweeps, spelling
   fixes, lint suppressions, and logging-level adjustments are each a
   separate commit, even when individually trivial.
2. **Imperative, scoped subject lines** — `module: short summary`. The
   body explains *why* and cites the requirement (`AU-3`, `Section 508
   §1194.21(b)`) when compliance-relevant.
3. **No `git add -A` after multi-area work.** Stage explicitly. If you
   are committing 15 files because that is what `status` showed, split.
4. **Drift cleanup is its own commit** — extend `.gitignore` and remove
   tracked copies separately after the feature commit, citing its hash.
5. **PR-level squash is not a substitute** — per-commit titles become the
   squash summary.

The only exception is a strict `git revert`, which is one commit by
definition.

